  "question": {
    "text": "Two isolated metallic solid spheres of radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></math> are charged such that both have same charge density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#963;</mi></math> . The spheres are then connected by a thin conducting wire. If the new charge density of the bigger sphere is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>&#963;</mi><mo>'</mo></msup></math>. The ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msup><mi>&#963;</mi><mo>'</mo></msup><mi>&#963;</mi></mfrac></math>&nbsp;is :",
    "image": null
  },
